  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how a sharp probe tip can interact with light and a nearby sample across a wide frequency range, illustrating the basic idea of tip-enhanced near-field spectroscopy. In the upper sketches, incoming light drives charge at the tip apex, creating a localized field between the tip and the sample. Depending on the geometry and sample response, the scattered light is modified as the tip approaches the surface.\n\nThe middle part emphasizes that when the tip is positioned over a nanoscale object, the optical interaction depends on the object’s internal polarization or dipole arrangement. The blue and white circles inside the dome-like particle suggest different charge distributions, and the outgoing wave differs accordingly, indicating that near-field scattering can reveal local optical modes or resonances that are not captured by far-field illumination alone.\n\nThe bottom strip places these mechanisms on the electromagnetic spectrum, from ultraviolet/visible through infrared and THz to microwave/radio frequencies. Small cartoons suggest different dominant interactions in each regime, such as electronic or molecular excitations at higher frequencies and coupled charge or dipole responses at lower frequencies. Overall, the image conveys that the same tip-based concept can probe nanoscale material properties over many decades of frequency.",
